Reserve a preview column on the right of the rich panel.

The launcher shows a name, a subtitle and an icon per row, which is all a row
has room for. Everything else a result could say (what the file actually looks
like, what a plugin's view would draw) had nowhere to go, so it went nowhere.

This adds the column that answers that, and deliberately does not fill it. The
renderer lays the list, its headings and the pill bar out inside what is left,
puts a hairline in the border colour on the boundary, and stops. Filling it is
the client's job, on a surface of its own.

That division is the whole point. A preview worth having is a laid-out
document, and a row painter has no way to become one. beamenu-canvas already
draws plugin views through WebKitGTK, so the same pane now draws previews.
Nothing here decodes an image or measures a paragraph, and nothing here has to.

The search row is untouched and still spans the whole panel, so the layout is
one query line above two columns rather than two panels side by side.

Two clamps, both in lib/renderers/preview.cpp so they are testable without a
compositor: the list never drops below BM_PREVIEW_MIN_LIST, and a column that
would come out under BM_PREVIEW_MIN_COLUMN after that is dropped rather than
drawn as a sliver. A panel too narrow for either degrades to the list-only
layout it had before this patch. `nix run .#beamenu-patch-test` covers the
arithmetic; nix/patches/beamenu/tests/preview_split_test.cpp is the driver.

bm_menu_get_panel_metrics exists because a client drawing over the panel
cannot work out where the panel put anything. The width comes from the output
size times the width factor. The height comes from what nesting did to the
rows. The split comes from the clamping above. Every one of those is decided
inside the renderer, and the end of the paint that used them is the only
moment all four numbers agree.

